CAT = "sgresMarket"; SEG = "MKT"
Q = [
("Which physical characteristic of real estate means a home cannot be moved to where buyers want it?",
 "Immobility", ["Durability", "Uniqueness", "Indivisibility"],
 "Immobility means a property's location is fixed, so location drives its value. Durability refers to land lasting indefinitely, not to its fixed position."),
("Compared with owning a condominium unit directly, holding units in a REIT is usually:",
 "More liquid and divisible", ["Less liquid and indivisible", "Free of any price risk", "Secured by a title in your name"],
 "REIT units trade on the stock exchange in small amounts, so they are easier to buy and sell than a whole property. The investor holds units, not a land title, and prices still move with the market."),
("Why is the real estate market described as an imperfect market?",
 "It has no central marketplace", ["A single agency sets every price", "All units are identical", "Units trade instantly online"],
 "Property has no central exchange, information is patchy and every unit differs, so prices are found deal by deal. That is what makes the market imperfect."),
("Under the Government Land Sales Programme, state land for private development is usually sold to developers by:",
 "Public tender", ["Balloting", "Private treaty", "Fixed-price allocation"],
 "Sites released under the Government Land Sales (GLS) Programme are generally sold by public tender, with the State awarding the site to a tenderer. Balloting is how HDB allocates new flats, not land."),
("HDB acquires state land for building public housing at:",
 "Fair market value", ["A nominal sum", "Reclamation cost only", "Half of market value"],
 "HDB pays the State fair market value for land used for public housing, assessed by the Chief Valuer. The housing subsidy is given to flat buyers through the flat price, not through cheap land."),
("Which agency sets the rules banks follow on housing loans, such as loan-to-value limits?",
 "MAS", ["URA", "IRAS", "SLA"],
 "The Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS) sets loan-to-value limits and the debt servicing frameworks for loans from financial institutions. IRAS administers taxes and SLA keeps the land register."),
("Which agency is Singapore's national land use planning authority?",
 "URA", ["SLA", "BCA", "JTC"],
 "The Urban Redevelopment Authority (URA) prepares the Concept Plan and Master Plan and grants planning permission. SLA manages state land and the land register; BCA handles building control."),
("Which government body is the main developer and landlord of industrial estates?",
 "JTC Corporation", ["HDB", "URA", "BCA"],
 "JTC Corporation develops and leases industrial land and premises. HDB develops public housing, while URA is the planning authority."),
("Which agency collects stamp duty and property tax?",
 "IRAS", ["CPF Board", "SLA", "MAS"],
 "The Inland Revenue Authority of Singapore (IRAS) administers stamp duty, property tax, income tax and GST. SLA keeps the land register but does not collect these taxes."),
("When interest rates rise sharply, demand for private homes generally:",
 "Falls as loans cost more", ["Rises as savings grow", "Stays fixed by law", "Rises across all segments"],
 "Higher rates raise monthly instalments and shrink how much buyers can borrow, so demand usually softens. Interest rates are one of the main macro factors in the property cycle."),
("Property cooling measures such as additional buyer's stamp duty are mainly meant to:",
 "Curb speculative demand", ["Raise HDB supply", "Cut bank interest rates", "Attract foreign buyers"],
 "Cooling measures target investment and speculative buying to keep prices stable and sustainable. They raise costs for some buyers rather than adding supply or lowering rates."),
("How does HDB price a new Build-To-Order flat?",
 "Market value less a subsidy", ["Construction cost only", "Lowest bid at tender", "The last nearby resale price"],
 "HDB starts from the flat's market value, set by valuation principles, then prices it below that with a subsidy. Housing grants can lower the buyer's cost further."),
]
